Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
russian foreign minister sergey lavrov  has said his country appreciates india  for not taking a quote one-sided view of  the situation in ukraine lavrov is in  india hoping to shore up a time-tested  partnership in the midst of crippling  economic sanctions over the war in  ukraine  this is what he said at a meeting with  indian foreign minister s joshankar  our western colleagues would like to  reduce any meaningful  international issue to the crisis in  ukraine  uh you know our position  we  do not hide  anything and we appreciate that india is  taking this situation in  the  entirety of facts not just  in a one-sided way  and earlier in the day indian foreign  minister s jaishankar met sergey lavrov  in delhi that's a tweet from sj  shankar's twitter account india's heavy  reliance on russian arms imports for its  military has forced india to abstain  from u.n resolutions censuring russia  both countries are also exploring a  currency mechanism that would allow  india to purchase cheaper crude oil from  russia sidestepping western sanctions  and earlier today foreign minister  lavrov said russia is willing to use the  indian rupee for international trade  amongst the indian public itself there  appears to be a lot of support for the  country's position on russia here's a  sample of the mood on the streets of  delhi  because we have a lot to gain from this  you know because uh  see like when you see that we are now  directly buying the oil from russia we  couldn't do that earlier like you know  and we can now do that  and we have a long-standing trade  relation like we know we buy our  military aircrafts and all from russia  we have this brahmos missile joint  venture with russia so i think  it's pretty good thing that like you  know that we are pretty vocal about our  support for russia now india's stand is  absolutely fine because they are  standing up for themselves which we  should be doing  and let's get more on this from garema  mohan she's a fellow at the asia program  at the think tank the german martial  fund garuma to quote u.s president joe  biden india is being seen as court  somewhat shaky on russia help us  understand where india stands right now  so india is  in a bit of a difficult position because  it has a long-standing partnership with  russia but one has to understand that  the nature of the india-russia  partnership has changed significantly  india's position right now of a study  neutrality does not mean that it  supports russian actions it just has a  different approach as to what to do to  solve the the current problem uh so  india had to take a more nuanced  position on this question because of  the location of indian students in  ukraine that needed to be evacuated 20  000 um indian students who were there  india's dependence on russia for  military equipment and technology which  a lot of other countries have not agreed  to  do in the past and russia has provided  certain equipment to india given the  crisis it faces with china on the border  which flared up in 2020 and hasn't  really gone away  this is playing a very important role in  india's calculus in the long term  however india's approach is that  isolating russia will probably not be  helpful it wants to  preserve whatever remnants of a leverage  it still has with moscow so russia does  not reach the conclusion that china is  its only partner in the world and india  wants to be able to retain the leverage  to to influence what's going on i have  to say that um it has been clear made  clear to us that in private india has  strongly criticized uh russia's actions  and even with the with the lavrov visit  today we saw some of that uh you you  talked about uh china a little while  earlier and  the partnership between russia and china  has been defined as a no limits  partnership by the leaders of both  nations uh how does that figure into new  delhi's view on ties with moscow  that is exactly uh the reason why new  delhi is so concerned  because  the main the key factor for india is the  threat from china and in case that this  gets worse  um india does not want russia to play  spoiler um inside with china and india  wants russia to play a somewhat uh  constructive role so this is of course  playing into india's calculations but  some have also noted that the  russia-china partnership is perhaps a  little bit overblown currently uh that  this is not an alliance and russia would  not want to be a junior partner to china  uh but of course india is watching this  development very closely and it is it is  worrying for for indian policy makers  right now  and uh of course uh garima delhi has  also faced a lot of criticism over its  plans to buy cheap russian crude oil in  the wake of the ukraine invasion and i'd  just like to play for our viewers an  exchange between visiting uk foreign  secretary lee struss and indian foreign  minister s joshankar last evening in  delhi let's just have a quick listen  ultimately it's oil and gas that is  funding putin's war machine and we need  to  cut off reliance on that and we need a  timetable to end reliance on russian oil  and gas so we need to do more and we  can't be complacent  now i was just reading a report today  that in the month of march europe has  bought i think 15 percent more oil and  gas  from russia than it did the month before  if you look at the major  buyers of oil and gas from russia  i think you'll find most of them are in  europe  very much just listening to that  exchange i suppose the main question  really is if india is right to purchase  oil from russia now given that it's  clear that this money will be used and  has been used to fund russian aggression  in ukraine  so i think another point that the indian  foreign minister made yesterday  in this exchange that you played perhaps  explains a little bit more the indian  position he said that india continues to  buy majority of its oil from the middle  east  seven to eight percent from the united  states and only about one percent from  russia  so i think for the for the indian  government which is you know the  countries just coming out of the  devastating um delta wave and the impact  of the kovit 19 pandemic is being keenly  felt uh by citizens by consumers oil  prices are sky high um for the  government of course it has to balance  both of these concerns but i think a  point that minister jay shankar is  trying to make is that this isn't  perhaps the strongest uh point to to  hold india to account to given that a  lot of europeans are also you know  continuing to buy this and um foreign  secretary trust made the point that this  is a long-term thing countries will have  to reduce dependence on actors like  russia and perhaps also china but this  doesn't happen overnight it's you know  it has to be a long-term thing  but despite that india is coming in for  a fair bit of criticism particularly  from its uh western partners on its uh  let's call it continued fence sitting  when it comes to uh  in india's position on russia is india  concerned that this might affect its  relations with its western partners  moving forward  don't think that india is actually  coming under criticism if you look at  the statements that  we saw from visiting foreign dignitaries  in the last week we had the uk foreign  secretary we had the foreign security  policy advisor to the german chancellor  mr plotner visit india his counterparts  counterpart from the netherlands visit  india and what i found interesting in  all of their statements was that  they said that would we like india to be  in our camp yes but we understand the  position that india is taking currently  and to quote mr plotner he even said  that india is in a particular  geopolitical situation itself  it has challenges it is facing in its  neighborhood and therefore we understand  the calculus that india has made i think  one thing india has done is that it has  made clear to partners in the west why  it is taking the position that it is  that it is choosing to publicly not call  out russia but it does not support  russian actions and even though it has  abstained in u.n votes it has issued  public statements explaining its  problems with the you know with the war  and the the the path that russia has  chosen to take even today with the  meetings after foreign minister lavrov  the indian foreign minister announced  that  any conflicts should be solved through  diplomacy dialogue respecting the u.n  charter international law sovereignty  and territorial integrity of all  countries now this is the strongest  statement you would get from from india  strongest official statement condemning  the war that you would get from from  india  uh if i can just get you to look into  your crystal ball uh gary ma and briefly  at that do you think moving forward  india uh will continue to remain neutral  as it were with regard to russia or is  that position likely to change  i think if we look at indian foreign  policy  post-cold war from the 50s onwards  it has always taken the position of not  publicly criticizing countries but  expressing its anger privately it  believes that isolating countries does  not leave room for change as a country  that had sanctions imposed on itself it  also has a different view of sanctions  so i think we have to be a bit careful  in characterizing the indian position  as fence sitting or neutral but  as we see particularly the russia china  relationship develop further um and  india  if it if it sees that it is not you know  um it is losing a bit of its leverage  with moscow of course india will have to  go back to the drawing board and and uh  you know rethink uh the decisions that  it is making  karima mohan from the german marshall  fund uh always a pleasure talking to you  thank you so much for joining us today  thank you  thank you for having me  you

